(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old man becomes ill over 4 days with a temperature of 40.1 °C (104.2 °F), shaking chills and confusion. He looks toxic, a new loud regurgitant murmur is heard, and painless hemorrhagic macules appear on his soles, which are Janeway lesions (painless, nontender spots). Which form of infective endocarditis fits his course?","choices":["Acute endocarditis","Subacute endocarditis","Libman-Sacks disease","Device endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Four days of illness with 40.1 °C (104.2 °F), shaking chills, confusion and a toxic appearance fit acute endocarditis: a hectically febrile illness from a virulent, invasive organism, with rapid onset typically over 3-10 days. The new loud murmur reflects rapid valve damage, and Janeway lesions show seeding beyond the heart. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who started amlodipine for hypertension five weeks ago has bilateral, symmetric ankle swelling without warmth, redness or tenderness. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Order a D-dimer","Review the medication as the cause","Bilateral leg ultrasound","Start therapeutic anticoagulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral, symmetric ankle swelling without warmth, redness or tenderness, beginning weeks after amlodipine, is the pattern of drug-induced edema. It is a common adverse effect of dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, so reviewing the medication as the cause is the logical next step. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which patient with recurrent pericarditis is described as a candidate for anti-interleukin-1 therapy?","choices":["One who is steroid-dependent","One in tamponade right now","One with a normal C-reactive protein","One with a first, mild episode"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent or colchicine-resistant disease, or steroid-dependent disease, makes anti-interleukin-1 therapy (anakinra or rilonacept) the preferred option. Current guidance also supports using it earlier when there is an inflammatory phenotype: C-reactive protein above 10 mg/L, fever or evidence of pericardial inflammation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with viridans streptococcal endocarditis had positive blood cultures on days 1 and 2 and her first negative culture on day 4. She asks how long her antibiotics will last. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["4-6 weeks counted from day 4","4-6 weeks from admission day","Until her fever settles","10 days from the first dose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotic therapy must be bactericidal and prolonged, typically 4-6 weeks counted from the first NEGATIVE culture. Her cultures were positive on days 1 and 2 and first negative on day 4, so day 4 starts the count, because all bacteria in the vegetation must be killed. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has three months of worsening breathlessness on exertion and ankle swelling. Dilated cardiomyopathy is suspected, but her 12-lead electrocardiogram is completely normal. Which interpretation of the normal tracing is most accurate?","choices":["It points to restrictive disease instead","It is what most dilated cardiomyopathy shows","It excludes every cause of heart failure","It makes dilated cardiomyopathy very unlikely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram is usually abnormal in dilated cardiomyopathy, with findings such as left ventricular hypertrophy, atrial fibrillation, premature ventricular contractions or left bundle branch block, so her completely normal tracing will almost rule it out. An echo with dilated, thin walls would be needed to support it. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with a diagnosed leg deep vein thrombosis, not yet treated, develops sudden shortness of breath and pleuritic chest pain. Which complication has occurred?","choices":["Pulmonary embolism","Post-thrombotic syndrome","Acute myocardial infarction","Septic thrombophlebitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Part of a deep vein thrombus can break off and travel to the lungs, causing a pulmonary embolism. Her untreated leg clot followed by sudden shortness of breath and pleuritic chest pain fits that complication. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man is newly diagnosed with restrictive cardiomyopathy caused by hemochromatosis, with iron deposited in the heart tissue. He has congestion and fatigue. Which is the first principle of his management?","choices":["Treat the underlying disorder","An inotrope to raise output","Septal myectomy for relief","A high-intensity exercise program"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of restrictive cardiomyopathy begins with treating the underlying disorder, here hemochromatosis. Diuretics are then used with caution for his congestion, beta blockers or verapamil and diltiazem may improve filling, and anticoagulants or a pacemaker are added as needed. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has repeated dizziness and near-fainting while climbing stairs. His electrocardiogram shows the PR interval lengthening until a beat is dropped, in a repeating pattern. He takes no atrioventricular nodal blocking drugs, and no reversible cause is found. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Intravenous amiodarone","Synchronized cardioversion","Reassurance and follow-up"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A PR that lengthens until a beat drops is Mobitz type I, and his dizziness and near-fainting on stairs fit its symptoms, which usually appear when exertion induces the block. With no nodal blocking drugs or reversible cause, a permanent pacemaker is considered for this symptomatic patient. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has sudden severe pain in his right leg. Pulses are absent below the knee. Which examination finding is often the earliest neurologic sign of acute vascular insufficiency?","choices":["Sensory loss over the foot's dorsum","Loss of hair on the shin","Complete paralysis of the leg","Absent patellar reflex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His sudden severe pain with absent pulses below the knee is acute limb ischemia. Light-touch sensory fibers are highly susceptible to ischemia, so sensory deficits over the dorsum of the foot are often the earliest neurologic sign of acute vascular insufficiency, which is why sensory testing is part of the examination. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Idarucizumab is the reversal agent for which anticoagulant?","choices":["Warfarin","Unfractionated heparin","Dabigatran","Apixaban"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Idarucizumab is the reversal agent for dabigatran when it causes life-threatening or intracranial hemorrhage, the most serious adverse effect of anticoagulation. Each anticoagulant group has its own reversal agent, so matching drug to antidote is the point. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with recurrent pericarditis over several years now has leg edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. Constrictive pericarditis is suspected. Which echocardiogram finding would best support it?","choices":["A dilated, weakly contracting ventricle","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","Right ventricular diastolic collapse","Septal bounce toward the left ventricle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Years of recurrent pericarditis, a listed antecedent of constriction, now with edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure fit constrictive pericarditis. A septal bounce toward the left ventricle is one of its echocardiographic features, along with increased pericardial thickness, pronounced respiratory variation in ventricular filling and moderate biatrial enlargement. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"After a positive D-dimer in the low likelihood Wells category, which test comes next?","choices":["Contrast venography","A repeat D-dimer","Stopping the work-up","Venous duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a low-likelihood patient, a negative D-dimer ends the work-up, but a positive one leads to ultrasound, because D-dimer is sensitive but not specific. A negative ultrasound then stops the evaluation, and venous duplex ultrasound has replaced contrast venography as the imaging test. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man arrives with a temperature of 40.2 °C (104.4 °F), rigors and a new murmur, and he looks toxic. Acute endocarditis is suspected. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Two cultures within 1-2 hours, then antibiotics","Observation for 24 hours without treatment","Echocardiography before any cultures","Antibiotics first, cultures tomorrow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His temperature of 40.2 °C (104.4 °F), rigors and toxic appearance suggest acute endocarditis, so 2 blood cultures are drawn within the first 1-2 hours rather than over 24 hours. Empiric therapy then starts, but ONLY after the cultures have been drawn. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 6-year-old with repaired congenital heart disease has 3 weeks of low-grade fever, fatigue, joint aches and weight loss. A new murmur is heard. Which organism most commonly causes endocarditis in this age group?","choices":["Enterococci","Candida species","Viridans group streptococci","Group B streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Children over 1 year with congenital heart disease develop indolent viridans group streptococcal disease (Streptococcus sanguis, mitis, mutans), with Staphylococcus aureus next. This 6-year-old's 3 weeks of low-grade fever, fatigue, joint aches and weight loss is that indolent course. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which organism is a classic cause of acute infective endocarditis?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans streptococci","Streptococcus gallolyticus","Gram-negative coccobacilli"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute infective endocarditis is caused by virulent organisms: beta-hemolytic streptococci, Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible or methicillin-resistant), sometimes Staphylococcus lugdunensis (a coagulase-negative staphylococcus), and enterococci. Staphylococcus aureus, a skin organism, is the classic acute cause. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man has known tachy-brady syndrome (a fast rhythm alternating with slow rates and pauses) and asks which fast rhythm is behind his palpitations. An ambulatory monitor is arranged. Which rhythm is it most likely to record during the fast episodes?","choices":["Junctional rhythm","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome, the form of sick sinus syndrome in which a fast rhythm alternates with slow rates or pauses, the tachyarrhythmia is most commonly atrial fibrillation. During his fast episodes the monitor would most likely show an irregularly irregular rhythm without P waves. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with acute pericarditis is starting ibuprofen and colchicine. He has a history of dyspepsia. Which addition to his regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["A prophylactic antibiotic","A proton pump inhibitor","An oral anticoagulant","A loop diuretic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ibuprofen and colchicine are the initial therapy for most acute pericarditis, and gastrointestinal protection is provided alongside anti-inflammatory treatment, for example a proton pump inhibitor such as omeprazole. His history of dyspepsia makes that protection especially relevant. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man receiving an unfractionated heparin infusion for deep vein thrombosis develops life-threatening bleeding. Which agent reverses the heparin?","choices":["Andexanet alfa","Protamine sulfate","Vitamin K by mouth","Idarucizumab"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He is on an unfractionated heparin (UFH) infusion and has life-threatening bleeding, the most serious adverse effect of anticoagulation. Protamine sulfate is the reversal agent for UFH and for low molecular weight heparin. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has an asymptomatic infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 3.4 cm on ultrasound. Which follow-up is most appropriate?","choices":["Imaging every 6 to 12 months","Ultrasound every three years","Elective repair now","No further imaging"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Once identified, an aneurysm needs routine follow-up of size and growth rate, with frequency set by size. His asymptomatic 3.4 cm aneurysm is in the 3.0 to 3.9 cm band, which is imaged with ultrasound every three years. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man who lives at home has 3 weeks of low-grade fever and a new murmur. He has had no recent hospital stay, procedure or urinary symptoms, and no primary source of infection is found. Two separate blood cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis. How do these cultures count under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["A minor criterion","A major criterion","Excluded as contaminants","Not counted until echocardiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Community-acquired enterococci in the absence of a primary focus count as a typical organism for endocarditis. He lives at home, has had no hospital stay, procedure or urinary source, and two separate cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis, so the major blood culture criterion is met. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man who smokes and has diabetes and hypertension asks whether he is too young to have peripheral artery disease. Which age threshold applies to him?","choices":["Age 70 with risk factors","Age 60 regardless of risk","Age 50 with risk factors","Age 40 with a family history"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age is a risk factor for peripheral artery disease at over 60 without other risk factors, or at 50 with risk factors such as smoking, diabetes, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, sedentary lifestyle or obesity. At 52 with smoking, diabetes and hypertension, he is not too young. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has two weeks of exertional breathlessness and chest pressure. Her electrocardiogram shows a left bundle branch block; a tracing from last year was normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Evaluate for heart disease","Synchronized cardioversion","Reassure: a normal variant","Place a pacemaker now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A left bundle branch block absent a year ago is new, and a new left bundle branch block should always be considered a sign of pathology, an ST-elevation myocardial infarction equivalent. With exertional breathlessness and chest pressure, she needs evaluation for underlying heart disease rather than reassurance. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has three months of progressive hoarseness. A chest radiograph shows widening of the mediastinal shadow. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Abdominal aortic aneurysm","Descending aortic aneurysm","Aortic arch aneurysm","Ascending aortic aneurysm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thoracic aneurysm symptoms depend on location, and an arch aneurysm causes hoarseness through the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Her long-standing hypertension is the common history, and the widened mediastinal shadow on chest radiograph is a typical finding. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has had 20 minutes of sudden palpitations. Her heart rate is 188, blood pressure 122/76, and she is alert with no chest pain or breathlessness. The electrocardiogram shows a regular narrow complex tachycardia with no visible P waves. Which is the most appropriate first step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous procainamide","Vagal maneuver","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sudden, regular narrow complex tachycardia at 188 with no visible P waves is supraventricular tachycardia, and with a blood pressure of 122/76, a clear mind and no chest pain or breathlessness she is stable. A vagal maneuver is first line, followed by adenosine, which may slow the rhythm enough to show P waves. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with hypertension and diabetes has nonvalvular atrial fibrillation and a high CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score. Which anticoagulant class is preferred?","choices":["Warfarin as first choice","Aspirin alone","No anticoagulant, given her age","A direct oral anticoagulant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke-risk) score is high (age 75 or older 2, female 1, hypertension 1, diabetes 1 = 5), so she typically requires anticoagulation unless bleeding risk is high. In nonvalvular atrial fibrillation the direct oral anticoagulants (dabigatran, apixaban, edoxaban or rivaroxaban) are preferred. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man with 3 months of weight loss presents with dyspnea and a large pericardial effusion. He has had no recent viral illness, chest surgery or radiation. Which etiology should be most strongly suspected?","choices":["Rheumatic fever","Malignancy","Coxsackievirus","Dressler syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In older adults there is a higher index of suspicion for malignancy, especially with an effusion or tamponade. At 81, with 3 months of weight loss, a large effusion and no viral illness, surgery or radiation to explain it, malignancy is the cause to pursue. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has scleroderma with Raynaud phenomenon, acid reflux, osteoporosis and long-standing pulmonary hypertension. Her electrocardiogram shows a right bundle branch block. Which of her conditions is a named cause of this block?","choices":["Raynaud phenomenon","Osteoporosis","Acid reflux","Pulmonary hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary hypertension is a named cause of right bundle branch block, alongside a sudden rise in right ventricular pressure from pulmonary embolism, ischemic heart disease, cardiomyopathies and degeneration. Her Raynaud phenomenon, reflux and osteoporosis are not on the list, and treatment depends on the underlying condition. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man has typical acute viral pericarditis with stable vital signs and no high-risk features. A transthoracic echocardiogram is ordered. Which result is most expected?","choices":["A normal study or only a small effusion","Apical ballooning with a normal base","A thick pericardium with septal bounce","Right ventricular diastolic collapse"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In typical acute viral pericarditis with stable vital signs and no high-risk features, the echocardiogram is often normal unless there is an associated effusion, so a normal study or a small effusion is expected. The chest radiograph is typically normal too, and most such patients need no invasive workup. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with chest pain and breathlessness after a recent viral illness is being evaluated for myocarditis. Her electrocardiogram shows sinus tachycardia. Which laboratory result would indicate cardiac myonecrosis?","choices":["A low natriuretic peptide","A low white cell count","Elevated serum troponin","Normal sedimentation rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In this young woman with chest pain and breathlessness after a viral illness, serum troponin is the indicator of cardiac myonecrosis. Her sinus tachycardia fits the electrocardiogram of myocarditis, and the rest of the work-up looks for leukocytosis and a possibly elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man has just been diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy on echocardiography. He has two younger sisters and a brother. His mother had unexplained sudden death at 44. Which step for his family is most appropriate?","choices":["No family evaluation is needed","Evaluate only female relatives","Test relatives only if symptomatic","Evaluate his first-degree relatives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most cases of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy are inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, in which one copy of the mutated gene from an affected parent is sufficient to cause the condition. His diagnosis and his mother's unexplained sudden death at 44 make evaluation of his siblings the right step. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has Mobitz type II block found on a routine tracing; she has no symptoms. Her family prefers to avoid procedures. Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Referral to cardiology","Permanent pacemaker placement","Observation without pacing","Reviewing her medication list"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Observation without pacing should be avoided: Mobitz type II is considered dangerous even without symptoms because of its unpredictable progression to complete block and poor prognosis, with a higher risk of sudden cardiac death. The definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker, even for a symptom-free patient. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Adenosine slows a tachycardia and reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White. Which drug is used?","choices":["A second dose of adenosine","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous atropine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Slowing from adenosine has unmasked atrial fibrillation conducting with Wolff-Parkinson-White, and in that setting intravenous procainamide is used, never adenosine. The same rule applies whenever Wolff-Parkinson-White is already known; adenosine stays first line only for ordinary orthodromic reentry. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is 4 days into his recovery from a large anterior myocardial infarction when he suddenly becomes hypotensive, with distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Constrictive pericarditis","Dressler syndrome","Massive pulmonary embolism","Free-wall rupture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sudden hypotension, distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds are the Beck triad of tamponade, and 4 days after a large myocardial infarction the listed cause that fits is free-wall rupture. Rapidly accumulating fluid causes tamponade early because the pericardium cannot stretch to accommodate it. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which electrical treatment is delivered for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Unsynchronized defibrillation","Transcutaneous pacing","Drugs first, shock later"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable, even when the patient looks fine, because it is a short-lived transitional state. It is treated with defibrillation (unsynchronized cardioversion), followed by urgent coronary angiography with possible revascularization. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman hospitalized after surgery has had a peripheral intravenous catheter in her arm for a week. She has a high fever, fluctuance over a tender vein, and redness spreading well past the vein. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Treat as infection within the vein","Compresses and ibuprofen with a recheck in 7 to 10 days","Start anticoagulation alone","Reassure that the process is self-limited"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High fever, fluctuance and erythema extending significantly beyond the vein margin suggest infection within the vein, suppurative or septic thrombophlebitis. Her week-long peripheral catheter is the expected setting, since septic thrombophlebitis is uncommon without a history of venous cannulation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has enterococcal endocarditis of the aortic valve after recurrent urinary infections. Which outcome is he at notable risk for?","choices":["Septic lung emboli","Needing heart surgery","Spontaneous cure","Lowest death rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His enterococcal aortic valve endocarditis is difficult to treat because of antibiotic resistance, and major complications with need for valve replacement occur in about 30-40% of patients. Needing heart surgery is the notable risk for this organism. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman who is 34 weeks pregnant sits in a car for nine hours and develops left calf swelling and pain. Her pregnancy supplies a hypercoagulable state and the long ride supplies venous stasis. Which additional component would complete the Virchow triad?","choices":["Venous stasis","Arterial thrombosis","Endothelial trauma","Hypercoagulable state"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The Virchow triad is venous stasis, a hypercoagulable state and vascular endothelial injury. Her pregnancy supplies the hypercoagulable state and nine hours in a car supply stasis, so the missing third is endothelial trauma, as from trauma, surgery, intravenous drug use or burns. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with symptomatic varicose veins has worn stockings and exercised faithfully for several months with persistent aching. Duplex ultrasound confirms great saphenous vein reflux. Which is the most appropriate next treatment?","choices":["Lifelong observation only","Venous ablation therapy","Oral flavonoid therapy","Surgical valvuloplasty"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A trial of conservative therapy with stockings is generally done for weeks to months before venous ablation therapy is offered. She has completed that trial with persistent aching and duplex confirms great saphenous reflux, so ablation, whose goal is better symptoms and appearance, comes next. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 35, 36, 41, 43"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man started a new blood pressure medication two weeks ago. He now feels tired, and his pulse is 46 and regular, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Thyroid tests are normal. Which medication is the most likely cause?","choices":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Lisinopril","Metoprolol","Amlodipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular rate of 46 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS is sinus bradycardia, and with normal thyroid tests the likely cause is a drug. Beta blockers such as metoprolol, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin are its listed drug causes, and metoprolol is the new blood pressure medication on that list. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has had an episode of fainting during basketball practice and brief runs of ventricular tachycardia on monitoring. She wants to keep playing on her competitive school team. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Unrestricted play with extra fluids","Activity restriction given her high risk","Clearance once the murmur softens","Competitive play on a beta blocker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her syncope during basketball identifies a patient at high risk of sudden death, and sudden cardiac death in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has its highest incidence in preadolescents and adolescents and is related to extreme exertion. Activity restriction for high-risk individuals is the adolescent strategy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 65, 66, 69, 76, 82, 105"},{"q":"What does the transthoracic echocardiogram show in Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Big atria, stiff normal ventricles","Septal wall of 1.5 cm or more","Four dilated, thin-walled chambers","Mid and apical wall hypokinesis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The transthoracic echocardiogram in Takotsubo cardiomyopathy shows a wall-motion abnormality: hypokinesis or akinesis of the midsegment and apical segment of the left ventricle, a shape that gave the syndrome its name after a Japanese octopus trap. Catheterization then confirms it, most often with normal coronary arteries. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after a motorcycle crash with a large thigh wound and heavy blood loss. He has no pulse. The monitor shows a regular narrow-complex rhythm at 110 per minute. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Asystole","Sinus tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","Pulseless electrical activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A regular narrow-complex rhythm at 110, one you would expect to produce a pulse, with no pulse is pulseless electrical activity. His heavy blood loss fits hypovolemia, one of its two leading causes in adults along with hypoxia, and the rhythm is unshockable, treated with compressions and epinephrine. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which aortic condition can also shed emboli that cause blue toe syndrome?","choices":["Pseudoaneurysm of the femoral","Aortic regurgitation","Carotid stenosis","Abdominal aortic aneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic, unruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm can produce embolic phenomena leading to blue toe syndrome, alongside abdominal, back or flank pain, isolated groin pain from femoral nerve pressure and early satiety. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 70-year-old smoker has a painful, dry, punched-out ulcer at the tip of his second toe with minimal bleeding and surrounding black necrotic tissue. It started as a minor scrape months ago. Which complication is he at risk of?","choices":["Osteomyelitis","Aortic dissection","Venous insufficiency","Pseudoaneurysm formation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A painful, dry, punched-out toe-tip ulcer that began as a minor scrape and has not healed for months, ringed by black necrotic tissue, is an ischemic ulcer of critical limb ischemia. Such ulcers fail because blood supply cannot meet the demands of healing tissue, are often surrounded by dry gangrene, and can lead to osteomyelitis. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy collapses briefly during a basketball game and recovers within a minute. Blood pressure and heart rate are normal. There is a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left sternal border in the fourth intercostal space that does not radiate to the carotids; it grows louder when he stands and softer when he squats. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Acute myocarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exertional collapse in an adolescent with normal vital signs and a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left sternal border that does not reach the carotids is obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. The murmur grows louder on standing (less preload) and softer on squatting (more preload), the outflow obstruction pattern. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman who had a left leg deep vein thrombosis after a hip fracture ten years ago reports a heavy, aching left lower leg by evening that eases when she elevates it. Examination shows pitting edema, shiny taut skin at the ankle and brownish pigmentation. Pedal pulses are strong. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic venous insufficiency","Peripheral artery disease","Lymphedema","Acute deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Progressive pitting edema of the lower leg with dull discomfort worsened by standing, shiny taut ankle skin and brownish (hemosiderin) pigmentation is the picture of chronic venous insufficiency. Incompetent valves keep blood from being pumped effectively out of the lower leg, and over time skin changes like hers follow. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"When is the dental prophylaxis dose given?","choices":["The evening after","One week before","30 to 60 minutes before","Daily for a month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every prophylaxis regimen is a single dose given 30-60 minutes before the procedure, whether oral amoxicillin, injected ampicillin, a macrolide, doxycycline or a cephalosporin. The drug and route change with allergy and the ability to take oral medication; the timing does not. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with long-standing hypertension has sudden, severe, tearing chest pain radiating to his back. Blood pressure is 184/106. Imaging confirms an aortic dissection. Which blood pressure goal applies?","choices":["Systolic above 140 mmHg","Systolic 100 to 120 mmHg","Systolic below 80 mmHg","No change until surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stopping progression of a dissection requires reducing blood pressure, with a systolic goal of 100 to 120 mmHg, using a beta blocker such as esmolol or a vasodilator such as nitroprusside. His pressure of 184/106 is far above that range, and pain management and surgical grafting follow. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has stable dilated cardiomyopathy of two years' duration with no systemic illness and no recent change in his heart function. He has read about heart biopsies online and asks whether he should have one. What is the role of a heart muscle biopsy for him?","choices":["Not routine and rarely useful","The gold standard for his disease","The first test before echocardiography","Required to confirm every case"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Endocardial biopsy is not used routinely in dilated cardiomyopathy and is rarely useful. Its indications are recent onset of rapidly deteriorating cardiac function or a history of systemic disease that can involve the heart, and this man has stable disease with neither. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man had his pacemaker generator changed 3 weeks ago. He now has a fever of 38.8 °C (101.8 °F), and imaging shows a vegetation on a pacemaker lead. Which organisms cause the largest share of this type of endocarditis?","choices":["Enterococci","Gram-negative coccobacilli","Viridans streptococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His vegetation is on a pacemaker lead 3 weeks after a generator change, which is cardiac implantable electronic device endocarditis. Coagulase-negative staphylococci cause the largest share of these cases (about 41%), followed by Staphylococcus aureus (about 36%). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman recovering well from her first episode of acute viral pericarditis asks how likely it is to come back. Which estimate is most accurate?","choices":["Under 1%","About 15 to 30%","About 60%","Over 90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent pericarditis affects about 15 to 30% of patients after an episode of acute pericarditis. Recurrence is therefore common enough to plan for, although most patients do not have another episode. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which causes should be checked first in a patient with frequent premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["Dehydration, fever","Hypoxemia, electrolytes","Hyperlipidemia, obesity","Hypertension, diabetes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Frequent premature ventricular contractions should prompt a search for their named causes: hypoxemia and electrolyte imbalance (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypercalcemia). These beats occur with or without structural heart disease, and alcohol, caffeine and stimulants are other triggers to ask about. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman on telemetry has torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). The first-line intravenous push broke the first run, but short runs keep recurring. She is alert with a pulse and a normal blood pressure. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide","Magnesium infusion","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades de pointes, and more magnesium is then given as an infusion. The push broke her first run, so with short runs recurring in an alert, normotensive patient, the next step is a magnesium infusion; amiodarone, procainamide and beta blockers are avoided. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with metastatic lung cancer has a symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion that has already reaccumulated once after drainage. Which consult is recommended to improve her outcomes and quality of life?","choices":["Physical therapy","Endocrinology","Palliative care","Rheumatology"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion carries a limited life expectancy, a median of 2 to 4 months, and often recurs within that time, as hers already has. A palliative care consult is recommended to improve clinical outcomes and quality of life. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure has atrial flutter (sawtooth flutter waves) with a ventricular rate of 140. She is alert, with a blood pressure of 124/78 and no chest pain. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","A beta or calcium channel blocker","Adenosine twice","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sawtooth flutter waves with a ventricular rate of 140 in a woman with right-sided heart failure are atrial flutter, and she is alert with a blood pressure of 124/78 and no chest pain, so she is stable. Stable flutter is rate controlled with a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker, though control is harder to achieve than in atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a rhythm strip in which a normal beat is followed by an early beat with an abnormal P wave and a narrow QRS, repeating so that every other beat is early (bigeminy). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Junctional rhythm","Ventricular bigeminy","Flutter with 2:1 block","Atrial bigeminy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each early beat has an abnormal P wave and a narrow QRS, which marks a premature atrial contraction beginning in the atria, and an ectopic beat every other beat is bigeminy. Premature atrial contractions are common in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, which fits this patient. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has recurrent atrioventricular nodal reentry episodes that tend to start after coffee, alcohol or decongestant (sympathomimetic) use. She has no heart disease. Which counseling is most appropriate?","choices":["Avoid exercise","Avoid her known triggers","Expect a pacemaker","Start an anticoagulant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nodal reentry may occur spontaneously or with exertion, caffeine, alcohol, beta-agonists or sympathomimetics, and her episodes follow coffee, alcohol and decongestants. Counseling her to avoid those known triggers fits a rhythm that is usually well tolerated, alongside vagal maneuvers she can use at home. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which medication is a recognized cause of pericardial effusion?","choices":["Metformin","Amlodipine","Levothyroxine","Doxorubicin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Doxorubicin is a listed medication cause of pericardial effusion, along with minoxidil, phenytoin, and hydralazine and procainamide through drug-induced lupus. Medications sit on the etiology list beside idiopathic, malignant, infectious, cardiac, metabolic, rheumatologic and traumatic causes. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What defines a transient ischemic attack?","choices":["Focal deficit lasting over 24 hours","Global confusion for one week","Headache without any deficit","Focal deficit lasting under 24 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A transient ischemic attack is a focal loss of neurologic function lasting less than 24 hours. Along with stroke, it classifies a patient with carotid artery disease as symptomatic when it has occurred within the last six months. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with heart failure has runs of 5 identical wide QRS beats on a Holter monitor. He has no symptoms during them. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Start oral amiodarone","Synchronized cardioversion","Optimize his heart failure care","Implantable defibrillator now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Runs of 5 identical wide beats on a Holter are non-sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, often seen with heart failure. Because he has no symptoms, management is optimizing treatment of his underlying cardiac disease, and the runs alone are not an indication for an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD). — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 43"}]};
